---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { loadContent } from "../../lib/content";
import { calBookingUrl } from "../../lib/site";

const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`Money and Credit | CBSE Class 10 Economics | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 Economics Chapter 3 study notes on Money and Credit. Barter system, double coincidence of wants, modern forms of money (currency & demand deposits), loan activities of banks, terms of credit, formal vs informal credit sectors, and Self-Help Groups (SHGs) with 20 board exam quiz questions."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Money and Credit
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · Economics Chapter 3</span>
      <h1>Money and Credit</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Explore financial systems: the evolution from barter to digital fiat, bank mediation between depositors and borrowers, the terms of credit, formal versus informal lending exploitation, and the empowerment of Self-Help Groups (SHGs).
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Foundational Monetary &amp; Credit Principles</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>💰 Money as a Medium of Exchange</h3>
          <p class="formula">Overcoming Double Coincidence of Wants</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Barter System:</strong> Direct exchange of goods without the use of money. Requires <strong>double coincidence of wants</strong>: what a person desires to sell is exactly what the other wishes to buy (e.g. shoe manufacturer trading shoes for wheat).</li>
            <li><strong>Money as Medium of Exchange:</strong> Eliminates the cumbersome requirement of double coincidence of wants by acting as an intermediate step in transactions.</li>
            <li><strong>Modern Forms of Money:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Currency:</em> Paper notes and coins. Unlike earlier commodity money (gold, grain, cattle), modern currency has no intrinsic value of its own, but is authorized by the Government of India and issued exclusively by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI).
              <br/>• <em>Deposits with Banks (Demand Deposits):</em> People deposit surplus cash in bank accounts. Deposits can be withdrawn on demand and transferred directly using <strong>cheques</strong>.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🏦 Loan Activities of Commercial Banks</h3>
          <p class="formula">Spread = Lending Rate − Deposit Rate</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Fractional Reserve Mechanism:</strong> Banks in India keep only a small proportion of their deposits as cash (about <strong>15%</strong> as Cash Reserve Ratio) to pay depositors on daily demand.</li>
            <li><strong>Lending Mediation:</strong> Banks use the major portion of the remaining 85% of deposits to extend loans for farming, commerce, education, and industry.</li>
            <li><strong>Bank Income (The Spread):</strong> Banks charge a higher interest rate on loans than what they pay to depositors. The difference between what is charged from borrowers and what is paid to depositors is the primary source of bank income.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>📝 Terms of Credit &amp; Debt-Trap</h3>
          <p class="formula">Four Elements: Interest Rate, Collateral, Documentation, Mode of Repayment</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Collateral:</strong> An asset that the borrower owns (land, house, vehicle, livestock, bank deposits) and pledges as a guarantee to a lender until the loan is repaid. If the borrower defaults, the lender has the legal right to seize and sell the collateral.</li>
            <li><strong>Two Different Credit Situations:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Positive Role (Salim the shoe manufacturer):</em> Credit helps meet working capital costs, complete production on time, and increase earnings.
              <br/>• <em>Debt-Trap (Swapna the groundnut farmer):</em> Crop failure forces sale of land to repay loan; credit pushes the borrower into a painful situation from which recovery is extremely difficult.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚖️ Formal vs. Informal Credit &amp; SHGs</h3>
          <p class="formula">Supervision by RBI &amp; Rural Empowerment</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Formal Sector:</strong> Commercial banks and cooperatives. Supervised strictly by the <strong>Reserve Bank of India (RBI)</strong>, which ensures banks maintain minimum cash balances and lend not just to profit-making businesses but also to small farmers and cottage industries. Fair and reasonable interest rates.</li>
            <li><strong>Informal Sector:</strong> Moneylenders, traders, employers, relatives, and friends. No organization supervises their credit activities. Exorbitant interest rates (often 36% to 60% p.a.), unfair terms, and vicious cycles of debt.</li>
            <li><strong>Self-Help Groups (SHGs) for the Poor:</strong> 15–20 rural members (especially women) pool small periodic savings (Rs 25–100). Members take small loans at nominal interest without collateral. Once regular in savings, the group becomes eligible for bank loans to establish micro-enterprises.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: The Banking Spread &amp; Formal vs. Informal Lending</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 1 · The Loan Activities of Commercial Banks</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Bank loan intermediation model">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Depositors Box -->
              <rect x="25" y="55" width="105" height="135" fill="#dcfce7" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/>
              <text x="77" y="80" fill="#15803d" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">DEPOSITORS</text>
              <text x="77" y="105" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">People make</text>
              <text x="77" y="120" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">deposits</text>
              <text x="77" y="145" fill="#166534" font-size="9" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Receive Interest</text>
              <text x="77" y="160" fill="#64748b" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">(e.g., 4%–5%)</text>

              <!-- Central Bank Box -->
              <rect x="175" y="45" width="110" height="155" fill="#eff6ff" stroke="#2563eb" stroke-width="2" rx="8"/>
              <text x="230" y="70" fill="#1d4ed8" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">BANK</text>
              <line x1="185" y1="80" x2="275" y2="80" stroke="#93c5fd" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="230" y="105" fill="#1e293b" font-size="9.5" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Keeps ~15%</text>
              <text x="230" y="120" fill="#475569" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Cash Reserve</text>
              <text x="230" y="145" fill="#b45309" font-size="9.5" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Lends ~85%</text>
              <text x="230" y="160" fill="#475569" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">as Credit</text>
              <rect x="180" y="172" width="100" height="20" fill="#dbeafe" rx="3"/>
              <text x="230" y="186" fill="#1e40af" font-size="8" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Profit = Spread</text>

              <!-- Borrowers Box -->
              <rect x="330" y="55" width="105" height="135" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="2" rx="6"/>
              <text x="382" y="80" fill="#b45309" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">BORROWERS</text>
              <text x="382" y="105" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">Take loans</text>
              <text x="382" y="120" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">for enterprise</text>
              <text x="382" y="145" fill="#b45309" font-size="9" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Pay Interest</text>
              <text x="382" y="160" fill="#64748b" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">(e.g., 9%–12%)</text>

              <!-- Flow Arrows -->
              <!-- Depositor to Bank -->
              <path d="M 130 95 L 175 95" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>
              <!-- Bank to Depositor Interest -->
              <path d="M 175 145 L 130 145" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-dasharray="3" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>

              <!-- Bank to Borrower Loan -->
              <path d="M 285 95 L 330 95" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>
              <!-- Borrower to Bank Repayment -->
              <path d="M 330 145 L 285 145" stroke="#d97706" stroke-dasharray="3" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Banks mediate between depositors with surplus funds and borrowers in need of productive credit.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 2 · Formal vs. Informal Lending Comparison</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Formal vs Informal credit sectors">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Formal Sector -->
              <rect x="30" y="35" width="185" height="185" fill="#f0fdf4" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="2" rx="8"/>
              <text x="122" y="62" fill="#15803d" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">FORMAL SECTOR</text>
              <text x="122" y="80" fill="#475569" font-size="9.5" text-anchor="middle">(Banks &amp; Cooperatives)</text>
              <line x1="45" y1="90" x2="200" y2="90" stroke="#86efac" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="122" y="112" fill="#166534" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">✓ Supervised by RBI</text>
              <text x="122" y="132" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">• Reasonable interest rates</text>
              <text x="122" y="150" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">• Collateral &amp; paperwork needed</text>
              <text x="122" y="168" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">• No physical harassment</text>
              <text x="122" y="195" fill="#15803d" font-size="9.5" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Promotes National Growth</text>

              <!-- Informal Sector -->
              <rect x="245" y="35" width="185" height="185" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="2" rx="8"/>
              <text x="337" y="62" fill="#991b1b" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">INFORMAL SECTOR</text>
              <text x="337" y="80" fill="#475569" font-size="9.5" text-anchor="middle">(Moneylenders, Traders)</text>
              <line x1="260" y1="90" x2="415" y2="90" stroke="#fca5a5" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="337" y="112" fill="#b91c1c" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">✗ No Supervising Agency</text>
              <text x="337" y="132" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">• High interest (36%–60%+)</text>
              <text x="337" y="150" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">• Unfair terms &amp; bonded labour</text>
              <text x="337" y="168" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">• Easy loans without collateral</text>
              <text x="337" y="195" fill="#b91c1c" font-size="9.5" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Leads to Painful Debt-Traps</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Why expanding cheap and affordable formal credit to rural poor and smallholders is vital for equitable economic development.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Board Exam Quiz Section (20 Questions) -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="quiz-header">
        <div>
          <h2>CBSE Board Examination Quiz</h2>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:0;">Test your understanding of Modern Money, Banking Operations, Terms of Credit, and SHGs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="quiz-score-badge" id="quizScoreBadge">Score: 0 / 20</div>
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-container" id="quizContainer">
        <!-- Rendered by JS -->
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-footer-actions">
        <button type="button" class="button" id="resetQuizBtn">Reset Quiz</button>
        <span class="small" id="quizProgressText">0 of 20 answered</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Call to Action Banner -->
    <section class="cta-banner">
      <h2>Ace Your Board Prep in Economics</h2>
      <p>Master formal vs informal credit models, collateral mechanics, and case studies with personalized mentoring.</p>
      <a href={calBookingUrl} class="button">Book a Doubt Clearing Session</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  const questions = [
    {
      q: "1. What is the fundamental condition required for a barter system to work?",
      options: [
        "Double coincidence of wants",
        "A functioning commercial bank branch",
        "A metallic coin standard",
        "Government price stabilization decrees"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "A barter system relies on double coincidence of wants, meaning both parties must agree to buy and sell each other's goods simultaneously."
    },
    {
      q: "2. Why is money called a 'medium of exchange'?",
      options: [
        "Because it is made of paper and metal",
        "Because it acts as an intermediate in the exchange process of goods and services",
        "Because it is exchanged only inside central banks",
        "Because it loses all value after 30 days"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Money facilitates transactions by eliminating the need for double coincidence of wants, acting as a universally accepted intermediary."
    },
    {
      q: "3. Why are modern paper currency notes accepted as a medium of exchange in India despite having no intrinsic value?",
      options: [
        "Because they are decorated with watermarks",
        "Because currency is authorized and legally backed by the Government of India",
        "Because currency notes can be eaten as emergency rations",
        "Because all notes are manufactured out of pure silver"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Modern currency is fiat money: it is accepted because it is legally authorized by the sovereign government as legal tender for settling debts."
    },
    {
      q: "4. Which institution in India has the sole authority to issue currency notes on behalf of the Central Government?",
      options: [
        "State Bank of India (SBI)",
        "Reserve Bank of India (RBI)",
        "Ministry of Commerce",
        "NITI Aayog"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Under the Reserve Bank of India Act, only the RBI issues currency notes on behalf of the Central Government of India."
    },
    {
      q: "5. What are 'Demand Deposits'?",
      options: [
        "Deposits in bank accounts that can be withdrawn by the depositor on demand",
        "Deposits that the bank demands from bankrupt companies",
        "Loans taken by state governments from the World Bank",
        "Compulsory savings deducted by private landlords"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Deposits with banks that account holders can withdraw at any time on demand are known as demand deposits."
    },
    {
      q: "6. What is a 'Cheque'?",
      options: [
        "A piece of metallic currency used for large international purchases",
        "A paper instructing the bank to pay a specific amount from the person's account to the person named on it",
        "A receipt issued by the police for traffic violations",
        "A lottery ticket issued by commercial banks"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "A cheque is a negotiable paper instrument directing a bank to transfer a specified sum directly from the drawer's account to the payee without physical cash."
    },
    {
      q: "7. Approximately what percentage of their total deposits do commercial banks in India hold as liquid cash?",
      options: [
        "About 15%",
        "100%",
        "50%",
        "Less than 1%"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Banks in India hold roughly 15% of their total deposits as cash reserve to meet the daily withdrawal requirements of depositors."
    },
    {
      q: "8. What is the primary source of income for commercial banks?",
      options: [
        "Foreign exchange grants from the International Monetary Fund",
        "The difference between the interest charged from borrowers and the interest paid to depositors (the spread)",
        "Direct income tax subsidies collected by district collectors",
        "Charging entry fees at the bank branch doors"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "The spread—charging higher interest rates on loans than what is paid on deposits—constitutes the main source of income for commercial banks."
    },
    {
      q: "9. What is 'Collateral' in credit agreements?",
      options: [
        "The monthly bonus paid to bank tellers",
        "An asset owned by the borrower (land, house, livestock) that is pledged as a security to the lender until the loan is repaid",
        "The interest rate penalty charged on early loan settlement",
        "A signed letter of recommendation from an elected MLA"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Collateral is an asset owned by the borrower pledged to the lender as a security guarantee against loan default."
    },
    {
      q: "10. Which four elements together constitute the 'Terms of Credit'?",
      options: [
        "Interest rate, collateral, documentation requirement, and mode of repayment",
        "Gold price, stock market index, currency color, and bank branch size",
        "Borrower's age, political party, religion, and caste",
        "Distance to the capital city, road conditions, rainfall, and fertilizer cost"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "The terms of credit comprise the agreed interest rate, collateral requirement, necessary documentation, and schedule/mode of repayment."
    },
    {
      q: "11. What is a 'Debt-Trap'?",
      options: [
        "When repayment of a loan pushes the borrower into a situation where recovery is impossible without selling productive assets like land",
        "A special discount coupon offered by credit card companies",
        "A locked room inside a bank vault",
        "When a bank fails to collect dividends on foreign bonds"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "A debt-trap occurs when a borrower takes credit that fails to yield income (e.g. crop failure), forcing them to borrow further or sell assets to repay, plunging them into chronic debt."
    },
    {
      q: "12. Which of the following is part of the 'Formal Sector' of credit in India?",
      options: [
        "Village moneylenders",
        "Agricultural traders",
        "Commercial Banks and Cooperative Societies",
        "Friends and wealthy relatives"
      ],
      correct: 2,
      explanation: "Commercial banks and cooperative credit societies constitute the formal credit sector, functioning under statutory regulations."
    },
    {
      q: "13. What is the role of the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) in supervising formal credit?",
      options: [
        "Ensuring banks maintain minimum cash balances and do not lend only to rich businesses, but also to small farmers and small-scale industries",
        "Deciding which individual citizens should be elected to Parliament",
        "Printing daily newspapers across state capitals",
        "Operating all village post offices"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "The RBI supervises banks to ensure financial stability, cash reserve compliance, and inclusive lending to priority sectors like agriculture and MSMEs."
    },
    {
      q: "14. Why is credit from the informal sector harmful to poor borrowers?",
      options: [
        "Informal lenders charge exorbitant interest rates, use coercive recovery methods, and leave borrowers with little surplus income",
        "Informal lenders demand all repayments in foreign currencies",
        "Informal loans are prohibited by international treaties",
        "Informal loans can only be taken by government employees"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Unregulated moneylenders charge very high interest rates (up to 36%–60% or more), plunging poor households into exploitation and debt-traps."
    },
    {
      q: "15. Why do poor households in rural India still depend heavily on informal sources of credit?",
      options: [
        "Bank branches are absent in many rural areas, and banks require collateral and extensive documentation which the poor lack",
        "Poor households prefer paying 60% interest rates",
        "Commercial banks are prohibited by law from opening rural branches",
        "Poor households are legally banned from entering bank premises"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Lack of physical bank presence, absence of collateral, and complex paperwork force poor rural families to turn to familiar local moneylenders."
    },
    {
      q: "16. What is the typical membership size of a Self-Help Group (SHG)?",
      options: [
        "15 to 20 members, usually belonging to one neighborhood",
        "500 to 1,000 members",
        "Exactly 2 members",
        "Unlimited members across the whole state"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "A typical SHG has 15 to 20 members, usually rural women who meet and save regularly."
    },
    {
      q: "17. How do Self-Help Groups (SHGs) help overcome the problem of lack of collateral?",
      options: [
        "Banks lend to the group as a collective entity based on regular savings track record without demanding individual collateral",
        "SHG members mortgage their voting cards to the government",
        "SHGs print their own paper money",
        "SHGs are backed by foreign venture capital"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Because the group takes collective responsibility for loan repayment, commercial banks extend collateral-free microcredit to registered SHGs."
    },
    {
      q: "18. Who takes decisions regarding loan activities, interest rates, and repayment terms in an SHG?",
      options: [
        "The district police chief",
        "The group members collectively",
        "The local moneylender",
        "The Reserve Bank Governor"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Decisions regarding savings amounts, loans granted, interest rates, and repayment schedules are taken democratically by the group members."
    },
    {
      q: "19. Which prominent Nobel Peace Prize winner founded the Grameen Bank of Bangladesh to pioneer microcredit for the rural poor?",
      options: [
        "Muhammad Yunus",
        "Amartya Sen",
        "Abhijit Banerjee",
        "Kailash Satyarthi"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Professor Muhammad Yunus founded the Grameen Bank in the 1970s, proving that poor women are creditworthy micro-entrepreneurs."
    },
    {
      q: "20. Why is cheap and affordable credit crucial for the country's economic development?",
      options: [
        "It encourages small farmers to invest in crops, allows small businesses to expand, generates enterprise, and prevents poverty traps",
        "It forces all foreign firms to shut down",
        "It eliminates the need for taxation",
        "It allows banks to avoid paying salaries to their staff"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Affordable credit stimulates production, enables entrepreneurship, raises farm incomes, and breaks the cycle of generational poverty."
    }
  ];

  function renderQuiz() {
    const container = document.getElementById('quizContainer');
    if (!container) return;
    container.innerHTML = '';

    questions.forEach((item, idx) => {
      const qCard = document.createElement('div');
      qCard.className = 'quiz-card';
      qCard.id = `qCard_${idx}`;

      const title = document.createElement('h3');
      title.className = 'quiz-question';
      title.textContent = item.q;
      qCard.appendChild(title);

      const optList = document.createElement('div');
      optList.className = 'quiz-options';

      item.options.forEach((optText, optIdx) => {
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.type = 'button';
        btn.className = 'quiz-opt-btn';
        btn.textContent = optText;
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => handleSelectOption(idx, optIdx));
        optList.appendChild(btn);
      });
      qCard.appendChild(optList);

      const feedback = document.createElement('div');
      feedback.className = 'quiz-feedback';
      feedback.id = `feedback_${idx}`;
      qCard.appendChild(feedback);

      container.appendChild(qCard);
    });
  }

  let userAnswers = {};

  function handleSelectOption(qIdx, optIdx) {
    if (userAnswers[qIdx] !== undefined) return;
    userAnswers[qIdx] = optIdx;

    const q = questions[qIdx];
    const isCorrect = (optIdx === q.correct);
    const card = document.getElementById(`qCard_${qIdx}`);
    const btns = card.querySelectorAll('.quiz-opt-btn');
    const fb = document.getElementById(`feedback_${qIdx}`);

    btns.forEach((btn, i) => {
      btn.disabled = true;
      if (i === q.correct) {
        btn.classList.add('opt-correct');
      } else if (i === optIdx) {
        btn.classList.add('opt-wrong');
      }
    });

    if (isCorrect) {
      fb.className = 'quiz-feedback feedback-correct';
      fb.innerHTML = `<strong>Correct!</strong> ${q.explanation}`;
    } else {
      fb.className = 'quiz-feedback feedback-wrong';
      fb.innerHTML = `<strong>Incorrect.</strong> ${q.explanation}`;
    }

    updateScoreSummary();
  }

  function updateScoreSummary() {
    let score = 0;
    const answeredCount = Object.keys(userAnswers).length;
    for (let qIdx in userAnswers) {
      if (userAnswers[qIdx] === questions[qIdx].correct) {
        score++;
      }
    }
    const badge = document.getElementById('quizScoreBadge');
    if (badge) badge.textContent = `Score: ${score} / ${questions.length}`;

    const prog = document.getElementById('quizProgressText');
    if (prog) prog.textContent = `${answeredCount} of ${questions.length} answered`;
  }

  function resetQuiz() {
    userAnswers = {};
    renderQuiz();
    updateScoreSummary();
  }

  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    renderQuiz();
    const resetBtn = document.getElementById('resetQuizBtn');
    if (resetBtn) resetBtn.addEventListener('click', resetQuiz);
  });
</script>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 960px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1rem 4rem;
  }

  nav.small {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  nav.small a {
    color: var(--brand);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .resource-header {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--brand);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-muted);
